[I was going to wait to post the newspaper article until I got permission from
Andrew Welsh-Huggins, the reporter who wrote it.  Seems like somebody jumped
the gun and did it, possibly without his or the Sunday Herald-Times permission.
But, since it's now out there in Electronica, I may as well post several
comments on it.  I've edited it as necessary to share my comments;  the
original full text is to be found in the newsgroup alt.censorship.  If Jacy
Daniels wants to cross-post it to comp.org.eff.talk and other newsgroups,
that's his business and he takes full responsibility for doing so.

Note that I've set the followups to the newsgroup comp.org.eff.talk, so if you
want to follow discussion, if any, be sure to subscribe to that newsgroup.]


In article jacy.daniels@dragonbbs.com (Jacy Daniels) writes:

>                        CULTURES CLASH ON COMPUTER
>                     Internet Users Debate Censorship
>
>In a borderless frontier, who sets the rules?  When cultures collide via
>computer, who picks up the pieces?...
>
>"That just opened the floodgates," he [Mike Regoli] recalled this week.  The
>person who originally posted the book [Fanny Hill] on Regoli's site, "got all
>these people in the 'alt.censorship group' just foaming."

It may seem like my earnestness to discuss this issue may have seemed like
a fomenting of the masses, but I did it to bring up very legitimate concerns
that needed to be dealt with -- NOW.  Mike knows that I did not send any e-mail
to CICA other than to him, and he may not know this, but I privately
discouraged people from sending any e-mail to his superiors as well (however,
I'd be surprised if no one attempted to talk to the Indiana University
administrators about this situation, but I don't know of anything specific --
Mike has kept quiet about any discussions he's had with CICA/IU staff).  

In addition, I highly admire and use the CICA archive site, and truly agree
that it is the supreme Windows archive site on the Internet, and probably among
all BBS (it compares to Windows On Line).  Mike has done an excellent job of
keeping this material organized and available, no small task.  Indiana Univ.
should be proud of what they've sponsored, even if they didn't actively
support it (and they SHOULD, if for no other reason great PR!).


>Regoli and others say the issue here was clearly selection, not censorship,
>since access to the book was not at stake:  there are hundred other places on
>the Internet where the book could be placed.

Here, I'd have to disagree.  First, his archive is so well-known that many
people can/will only access CICA for their Windows needs (they may not read
Usenet, for example, and probably aren't experienced Internet travelers, so
how would they know where else to look?  How would they even know a particular
application exists? -- I wouldn't be surprised if most people who access CICA
are mainly "fishing" for some kind of application(s), but don't know the
specifics.)  Second, his archive is also "gatewayed" to outside of the
Internet via a CD-ROM, and possibly BBS sites that mirror his archive (Mike,
if you could clarify this, I'd appreciate it).

Thus, as a distributor of a Windows application (yes, Windows Help file
hypertext documents are Windows applications!), I'd like to be able to use
the *unique* market that CICA has established, to market/distribute all of my
Windows hypertext applications (of which "Fanny Hill" is one -- I have others
waiting in the wings, most of which should not elicit any censorship
reaction except from the wierdest of groups).  It is a matter of fairness and
equal treatment --> that's all I ask for.

However, it is clear Mike *reactively* rather than *proactively* removed
Fanny Hill for the reason he got several e-mail complaints about its *content*
and NOT because he had concerns whether or not Windows Help files can be
considered Windows applications per his archives definition of what
constitutes a Windows application.  As I said above, Mike would have to
present a darn strong argument to show that Windows Help files of any kind
cannot be considered a Windows application, especially when you look at a lot
of the stuff that is in the archive already (I'm not sure if my Aesop's Fables
is there now since I uploaded that at about the same time -- never bothered
to look -- maybe I will soon).

I do empathize with him, since it's possible I might have taken the same path
he did if I were in his shoes "there but for the Grace of God go I".  But just
because two people may choose a certain road doesn't make it the right road.
Hindsight is better than foresight -- let's bury the hatchet and take the next
positive step of benefit to all.


>The novel, by John Cleland, is a first-person narrative describing the
>adventures of the fictitious Englishwoman Fanny Hill.  Although the material
>is clearly adult in nature, scholars have also recognized it as a work with
>genuine literary qualities.

This is actually the most accurate view of "Fanny Hill" I've yet seen.  I
should incorporate this into the Introduction.  :^)


>Regoli, managing editor of the "Organization of American Historians magazine,
>sees a certain irony that people brand him a censor when he removes that book,
>but don't say a word because his site won't take non-Windows software programs.

Well, as I said above, this is his judgement, but it is hard to justify given
the facts of what constitutes a Windows application as well as what's already
in the archive.  Removing something for *content* reasons rather than
*applications/technical* reasons are two totally different animals.


>He also wonders how far this movement can go.

I certainly hope that it doesn't mean the end of the CICA archive -- I
totally support Mike's efforts in keeping this archive going strong.  It would
be a BIG-TIME mistake for Indiana University to close it down since it is so
highly thought of by tens or even hundreds of thousands of people who've used
it.  I do believe he should seriously consider implementing a form of the ALA
statement, which effectively says (my interpretation -- Carl Kadie, please
comment) that his archive would not remove any Windows application that is
uploaded (and Window's application needs to be strictly defined) for *content*
reasons, except where the content clearly goes beyond Constitutional
protection.  Fanny Hill, for example, was declared "not obscene" by the U.S.
Supreme Court in 1966.  Thus, I believe that Fanny Hill, the Windows Help
file version, should be archived at CICA (and distributed on its CD-ROM)
because it is  1) protected by the U.S. Constitution, and  2) is a Windows
application of interest to many.

I encourage you, Mike to talk to CICA/IU staff to draft a forward-looking
policy statement regarding what will be accepted and what won't.  I don't
believe the current one meets the ALA standard by a long-shot.  I'm sure Carl
Kadie and the EFF have good ideas as to how it should be written.  Once you
have it, then your site will become immune to almost all content-related calls
for censorship that could be directed your way.  So when the "New Zealand
Censorship Society" sends you obnoxious e-mail demanding censorship of a
Contitutionally-protected work, their e-mail will roll off of you like water
on a duck, and you will have the full support from the University.  So, be
brave, Mike, make it happen.  We're here to help you.


>One of the programs in the archives is a version of the Bible in hypertext, a
>program that allows non-linear examination of texts.  "I'm getting calls to
>remove that," he said.

I think some of these calls are by people who are upset that "Fanny Hill" was
removed from CICA, and this is their form of "an eye for an eye".  You have
my total support to continue to archive at CICA the Bible, and in the future
any other controversial works such as the Qu'ran (I'm thinking of hypertexting
that!), Mein Kampf (it's *disgusting*, I know, but I believe in NO CENSORSHIP
of Constitutionally protected works and it is protected, last I looked), and
just about anything else at CICA.  I urge everybody to support and encourage
Mike and IU to draft the right kind of anti-censorship policy statement for
uploading and archiving to the CICA Windows archive.


>Another irony is that Regoli runs the site practically as a volunteer, after
>his duties as magazine editor and PC systems administrator for the historians
>organization on North Bryan Street.

As I said before, I'm quite impressed by what Mike has done.  I encourage him
to rise above this hub-bub and become an anti-censorship advocate.  He's in
a unique position to set the standards for the whole Internet to follow as it
becomes the seed of the World Information Super-Highway (WISH -- you heard it
here first!).  If we let the "New Zealand Censorship Society" and "Mothers
Against Saying the Word Nooky-Nooky" get their way with your archive, it sets
a VERY BAD precedent -- and I mean BIG-TIME BAD.  That's why I'm spending the
time posting on this subject as I do.


>As the Internet grows, the temptation to avoid such incidents by controlling
>the network will also increase.  And that could spell disaster for the
>Internet just as it reaches its period of greatest growth, warns Ost.

I totally agree.  I hope that CICA/IU sees it this way and implements an anti-
censorship policy at the CICA archive that would be the model for everyone
else to follow.  Mike, you and Indiana University are at a unique place in
history -- seize the opportunity, the moment.  You've got a lot of people out
here in Electronica waiting to help you draft a strong and air-tight anti-
censorship policy.  And once it's drafted, you'll be guaranteed to have a lot
of support from thousands out here on the net who are waiting to slay the
next dragon of censorship (sp. Newzealandus censorshipus var. conservativicus,
or Jerryfalwellus fundamentalus) that breathes on CICA/IU.


>"Overregulating the Internet will kill it.  And that's what people are
>worried about," Ost said.

Very worried.  If CICA/IU takes the forward-looking stand and drafts a strong
anti-censorship policy for the archive, then that will go a long way towards
preventing this from happening.  However, if Indiana University drops the
ball here at a unique time in history, I see even bigger storm clouds brewing
on the horizon for them and for all other archive sites and the Usenet.


>Noring understands that people like Regoli often choose against including
>material they receive on Internet sites, for a variety of reasons.  He also
>recognizes that unofficial administrators, often working private and without
>pay with the consent of an employer, are common in the Internet universe...
>
>But as a strong opponent of any form of censorship, Noring believes removing
>something for technical reasons is different than pulling something because
>of concerns about content.
>
>"If they do use editorial judgment and say, "Well, it looks kind nice, but we
>don't want it,' that's one thing.  But they took it off because they got
>e-mail...from someone in New Zealand who didn't like it," said Noring.

As Andy Warhol said, we all get our ten (or fifteen) minutes of fame -- I've
used up about 5 minutes already.  :^)

I apologize to those down under who feel I'm picking on New Zealand.  I love
the country and want to visit it someday.  I keep in e-mail touch with several
from there who I highly admire and am proud to call my net-friends.  And I'm
sure there are many netters from New Zealand who are aghast at what's happened
at CICA.  But, I've received several e-mails from New Zealanders who supported
the censorship of Fanny Hill by CICA, more than from any other country!  Is
there something going on in your country that I don't know about?  Please,
help me restore faith in your country!


>The debate over Regoli's action brings to mind an earlier historical moment,
>said Sue Steger, director of policy and planning for IU's Office Information
>Technology.
>
>"It's a whole new culture out there," she said.  "A good analogy is the Wild
>West."
>
>What happened to Regoli illustrates perfectly the growing pains of computer
>networks available to almost anyone anywhere with a computer and modem.
>
>A new community has developed that's not geographically based, she said.  Yet
>individuals from those communities -- from Iceland to Indiana bring their own
>cultural standards to the Internet.
>
>What might not raise a brow in one place is outlawed pornography another.
>
>Then, "you have someone like Mike having to run a little U.N. session," she
>said.
>
>Steger cites an incident last semester in which an Australian official
>contacted her after learning that a female IU student was transmitting
>pornography to a friend in Australia.
>
>Steger called the student in and explained the situation, leaving the
>decision up to her.  The student was shocked her action had caused such an
>uproar.
>
>"It reminded me of students traveling abroad without realizing different
>countries have different standards," Steger said.  "When you're traveling on
>the Internet, you have to be aware of different standards."

Good observation.  Remember, though, that the CICA archive is *IN* the United
States, not New Zealand, and subject to the U.S. Constitution.  We cannot
allow censorship of Constitutionally-protected materials to ever succeed, and
DOUBLY so when the pressure is exerted from culturally/religiously/politically
different lobbying groups *outside* the U.S.!  Are we going to let people from
outside the U.S. interpret our Constitution for us?  Let them control their
archives their way, we'll control ours our way.  That's another reason why
this incident is VERY SERIOUS, since all the visible complaints against Fanny
Hill at CICA came from New Zealand, and they succeeded!  As far as I am
concerned, the complaints to CICA/IU from outside of the U.S. should not have
been taken into consideration in the decision by Mike to remove "Fanny Hill".
I could say more on this subject but the overall negative tone it would
engender would divert from the main message of this post.


Anyway, Ms. Steger seems like a very reasonable person to talk with about
drafting a strong anti-censorship policy for the CICA archive, maybe with
help from EFF, that could be used as a model for others to follow.  Such a
policy is VERY necessary for the integrity of the future WISH.  Does anyone
know her e-mail address?  I'd like to send this post to her.


