obligations on Mike [the CICA archvist].  That is, given that he's
operating in a purely volunteer role."]

Newsgroups: comp.org.eff.talk
From: greg@bronze.ucs.indiana.edu (Gregory R. TRAVIS)
Subject:  Re: Fanny Hill Rides Again! (was Re: Censor on Internet..Pt-1)
Message-ID: <CKMFqD.D2D@usenet.ucs.indiana.edu>
Date: Wed, 2 Feb 1994 23:54:12 GMT

In <noringCKM6Ep.Hu2@netcom.com> noring@netcom.com (Jon Noring) writes:

>If this were Mike's site, he could do anything he pleases with it, and I'd
>support his right to do that.

How does your assesment that it's "not Mike's site" jive with the fact
that he could simply walk away from it without fear of losing his
job or be subject to possible criminal proceedings?

Through all this, it's not been made clear to me how the University's
obligations vis-a-vis the Constitution translate into obligations on
Mike.  That is, given that he's operating in a purely volunteer role.

Now, you could argue (as I'm sure that many will) that once he uses
State Property (TM), that he's obliged to abide by the same rules
as the overall organization.  However, I see a fundamental tension in
that argument vis-a-vis academic freedom.

For instance, a professor at I.U. is given a fine office and furniture
for his or her use.  That professor is then allowed to control access
to the office and the content of materials in the office.  There is
no question as to ownership of the office or the furniture - the
state owns it.

You could argue that the FTP site is not like an office - that it's
much more like a library (in the tired old analogy).  However, unlike
a library, and much like a professor's office, access to FTP sites
is commonly restricted to certain times of day and by number of
concurrent users.  Furthermore, the content of the site is often much
like a professor's office in that materials are very topic-specific.

Finally, like a professor's office and unlike I.U.'s library,
access is strictly limited to those who can afford it.  The average
Joe, who works at Denny's (say) and cannot afford to enroll at I.U.,
has no right to take up the professor's time.  That same average
Joe, who cannot afford commercial access to the Internet, has no
legal right to CICA's FTP archive.  He can, however, go into
I.U.'s library where they will NOT give him Internet access, but will
allow him to peruse the stacks all day long.

Are you willing to say that an English professor, who teaches
English literature and is known to loan his or her books to students,
is bound by the Constitution to carry a copy of "Fanny Hill" if
someone requests it?

>It is not, however, Mike's site, it's a public
>institutional site, supported by state and federal dollars, as long as Mike
>uses the equipment, and/or the name of CICA, or IU in describing it.  For it
>to *truly* be Mike's site, he'd have to buy or rent his own computer and pay
>for his own feed, etc.

What if that same English professor starts a windows FTP site, using
the PC that his department donated?  How about if a student
calls up and wishes to know about Fanny Hill?  Is the professor, a civil
servant at work during his or her office hours, bound by law to discuss
the book he or she has, that's sitting on a publicly-provided
bookshelf, in a publicly-provided office, over a publicly-provided phone?

If not, is the same professor obligated to loan out the book to any
student that requests it, assuming that the book was purchased with state
funds?

If not, is Mike obligated to loan out Fanny Hill if he moves
ftp.cica.indiana.edu into his office?

>It is clear that Mike removed Fanny Hill because he
>feared that his archive could be closed down by those at CICA/IU who, he
>perceived, didn't want to be bothered with complaints.

So, are you saying that being timid should be illegal?

Now, you can argue that Mike shouldn't have been timid, that
self-interested self-censorship is not a valid excuse for what
he did - I might agree with you.  However, to force him to do as YOU
want, in the name of the First Amendment seems to be terribly
disingenuous.

I appreciate your attempts to control his
behavior based upon the fact that he's using state-owned equipment. 
However, I think that enough examples can be found in which individuals,
employed by the state and using state-owned equipment, are not
subject to constitutional restraint that it'll be difficult to bind
Mike to the same restraints.

Here's a question for Carl Kadie, or perhaps counselor Goodwin:  What's
the legal rational for binding state institutions, such as a university,
to constitutional restraints?  I can imagine a state, or the university
itself, adopting a "no censorship" law, but what's the Constitution
got to do with it?  I realize it must have something to
do with the Fourteenth Amendment and/or the receipt of federal
funds but...

How far do those restraints go - for example:  Why ISN'T a professor,
who obviously receives state and federal funds (in the form of a
salary), not bound by the First Amendment (i.e. why is he or she
allowed to choose a religion)?  I don't buy that they're allowed to do
so on "private" time because even then they're expending public
funds (again, their salary).  This is a serious question - I'm not trying to
bait anyone.

>Thanks for sharing the nuances of the U.S. Constitution.  Last I heard, the
>Supreme Court ruled that Fanny Hill was "protected" material under the
>Constitution -- that's good enough for me.

All that says is that you, me, or Mike cannot be punished for publishing
Fanny Hill as private individuals.  It says NOTHING about you, me, or Mike
being OBLIGATED to publish it as private OR public individuals.

>In libertarian heaven, the "Forces of Censorship" cannot
>exist, but this is the Real World (tm), and they carry a lot of power and they
>are anti-libertarian, and they'll go after the private sites as well.

In "libertarian heaven" Indiana University is a private entity and
may choose to publish or not publish any damn thing they want, regardless
of content or reason.  In "libertarian heaven" Mike's actions would
be construed as a totally rational attempt at self-preservation.  And,
when Indiana University fired Mike for having "Fanny Hill," in
"liberatarian heaven," all the libertarians would applaud a private
concern's exercise of its rights and tell Mike to go start his own
university if he wanted that kind of crap around.

